      ":question/math-academy-id": "e-467",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/4} \\dfrac{\\tan x \\sec^2 x}{1+\\tan^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u = 1+\\tan^2 x.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi/4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x{sec}^{2}\u2061x}{1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}\u2061x{sec}^{2}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612-\\operatorname{ln}\u20611) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u20612\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
    ":knowledge-point/id": "fc24eade-1b3d-5672-88fd-0bb48b371509",
